  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nOur stretch of quiet and dry weather is at the beginning of the \nend. An expansive surface high pressure over the Great Lakes \nregion will slowly drift toward the Mid-Atlantic this weekend. \nThis will keep the region dry and temperatures mild for Halloween.\nSaturday will be pleasant. Winds will gradually shift from \neasterly to southeasterly as mid to high level cloud deck builds \nin. This will support a mostly cloudy day with filtered sunshine -\na treat for our trick-or-treaters! Temperatures will top out in \nthe low to mid 70s Saturday afternoon with temps falling into the \nupper 50s Saturday evening. It'll remain dry with near-zero chance\nof rain. \n\nA weak shortwave will track from the southern Plains into the region \non Sunday. This will lead to an increase in moisture ahead of the \ndisturbance, but the residual dry air in place from the long stretch \nunder the influence of the high pressure will limit precipitation \nchances until late Sunday into Monday. The primary precipitation \nmechanism is tied to broad isentropic lift that should slowly \nsaturate the dry air and support isolated to scattered showers late \nSunday into Sunday night. Any rain that falls will be generally \nlight and insignificant given the low level dry air eating into \nsensible perception. \n\nThe first week of November will bring a legitimate pattern change \nacross the CONUS. Guidance support a more amplified look that'll \nlead to more precipitation chances locally. For Monday & Tuesday, \nridging will build in and return the familiar warm & dry weather \nback to the region. Upstream, a potent trough will dig into the \nPlains and begin the slow trek eastward. This system and the \nassociated surface cold front arrives sometime Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning and will bring much needed rain across the TN \nValley. While instability looks rather meager at this time (limiting \nsevere potential), sufficient synoptic forcing along the pre-frontal \ntrough should support some thunderstorms and widespread rainfall. \nBehind the front Thursday into Friday, northwest flow aloft will \nusher in cooler air that will support more seasonable temperatures \nfor the end of the work week.",
